{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba README.md | sed -n '288,304p'\"","aggregated_output":"   288\t| Large refactor | Long interview (40+ questions), many tasks, full validation, draft PR |\n   289\t| Overnight build | Same as above, run in tmux |\n   290\t\n   291\t## How it was built\n   292\t\n   293\tThe first version was hand-written during a `/grill` session that produced the design decisions and shared vocabulary. The second version was built by Darkfactory itself -- Claude workers generated the skills from the specs, and a Codex code review caught issues that a fix worker addressed. Everything since then has been iterative improvement, and the system is still actively evolving.\n   294\t\n   295\t## Key references\n   296\t\n   297\t- [Architecture](ARCH.md) -- living architecture document covering the three-role agent model, information walls, build loop, and planning constraints\n   298\t- [Factory Missions talk](docs/reference_factory_talk.md) -- the multi-agent architecture that inspired this project\n   299\t- [Matt Pocock skills talk](docs/reference_pocock_talk.md) -- design alignment, ubiquitous language, and TDD patterns\n   300\t- [Provenance](docs/provenance.md) -- how Darkfactory relates to both sources: what's faithful, what's different, and what's new\n   301\t- [Design decisions](docs/design-decisions.md) -- every architectural choice with rationale\n   302\t- [Ubiquitous language](UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md) -- shared terminology used throughout the codebase\n   303\t\n   304\t## License\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQE4EJ2EGRYEET558ZVNHM9F","ts":"2026-04-30T02:46:44Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba README.md | sed -n '68,86p'\"","aggregated_output":"    68\t\n    69\tThe orchestrator is the main Claude Code session that runs your mission. It starts by interviewing you about what you want to build, asking pointed questions until it understands your goals, constraints, and edge cases. It then creates a shared glossary of terms so that you, the orchestrator, and all workers use the same vocabulary.\n    70\t\n    71\tFrom that conversation, it writes a PRD (Product Requirements Document) -- a structured description of the problem, the solution, user stories, and testing approach. The PRD is filed as a GitHub issue and becomes the source of truth for everything that follows. At the end of the mission, the draft PR links back to this issue.\n    72\t\n    73\tNext, it creates a series of planning documents using [OpenSpec](https://github.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec) (an open-source tool for structured change proposals). These documents are created one at a time, and you review each before the orchestrator moves on:\n    74\t\n    75\t- **Proposal** -- what you're building and why\n    76\t- **Specs** -- detailed requirements written as WHEN/THEN rules (e.g. \"WHEN a user submits the login form with wrong credentials, THEN show an error message\")\n    77\t- **Design** -- architecture decisions and technical approach. The design evaluates 2-3 architecture candidates against a four-dimension rubric (separation of concerns, testability, task independence, module depth) and picks the highest-scoring option. A mandatory gate verifies the design includes an architecture decision, module inventory, type catalog, error strategy, integration points, and test strategy; the Type Catalog names problem-space domain types and excludes technical helpers like CLI parsers, test fixtures, and generic containers.\n    78\t- **Tasks** -- an ordered checklist of implementation work. Before generating tasks, the orchestrator presents a decomposition strategy (layered, vertical, or hybrid) with a target task count for your approval. Each task includes topology metadata -- which prior tasks it depends on, what it consumes, and what it produces.\n    79\t\n    80\tEvery tasks.md ends with a set of **mandatory closing tasks** that run after the feature work: a smoke test (one end-to-end test through the primary user flow against real dependencies), a cleanup task (remove dead code, unused deps, and leftover scaffolding), a module shape re-check (catch drift between design.md and what the code actually exposes, including Type Catalog domain type names -- required once a mission has 5+ feature tasks), a documentation task, and a CLAUDE.md + ARCH.md generation task (creates project convention files if they don't already exist). A CI configuration task is optional and added when relevant.\n    81\t\n    82\tAfter the tasks are defined, the orchestrator runs a **coverage audit**. This maps every requirement from the PRD to one or more tasks. If anything is missing, it flags it. If a requirement was intentionally changed or dropped, you approve that decision explicitly. For substitutions, the orchestrator also checks that the replacement approach is fully covered by existing tasks -- if the substitution drops something important, it's flagged as a gap. Nothing falls through the cracks by accident.\n    83\t\n    84\tContract assertions trace back to specific spec capability and scenario identifiers for end-to-end traceability.\n    85\t\n    86\tFinally, it writes a **contract** -- a checklist of things the finished code should do, written so they can be tested from the outside (by running the code, not by reading the source). For example: \"the login endpoint returns a 401 when given wrong credentials.\" Each item traces back to a specific spec scenario and is tagged with how it should be verified (by executing the code, checking its output, or inspecting the source). Before moving on, the orchestrator runs a testability check to catch any vague or untestable items early. You review the contract, confirm which AI models handle each role (see [Configuration](#configuration)), and approve launch.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
