{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba skills/mission/templates/design-template.md | sed -n '55,85p'\"","aggregated_output":"    55\t\n    56\t## Module Inventory\n    57\t\n    58\tList each planned module or major component. Use this table format:\n    59\t\n    60\t| Module name | Interface width | What it hides | Why this boundary |\n    61\t|-------------|-----------------|---------------|-------------------|\n    62\t| `<module>` | Narrow / moderate / broad, with the public entry points named | Implementation details callers do not need to know | Reason this responsibility belongs behind this boundary |\n    63\t\n    64\t## Type Catalog\n    65\t\n    66\tList domain types only. Type names must come from `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` when the term exists there. Do not include field-level detail.\n    67\tDomain types model the problem space; exclude technical helpers such as CLI parsers, test fixtures, and generic containers unless they are public compatibility surfaces.\n    68\t\n    69\t| Type name | Purpose | Owning module |\n    70\t|-----------|---------|---------------|\n    71\t| `<type>` | One-line purpose | `<module>` |\n    72\t\n    73\t## Error Strategy\n    74\t\n    75\tDefine:\n    76\t- Error type hierarchy\n    77\t- Propagation rules\n    78\t- Which layer throws each error category\n    79\t- Which layer catches and translates each error category\n    80\t\n    81\tKeep this at the design level. Do not list every possible error instance unless the specs require it.\n    82\t\n    83\t## Integration Points\n    84\t\n    85\tDescribe how modules call each other. Use this table format:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
