{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ARCH.md | sed -n '1,120p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Architecture\n     2\t\n     3\tLiving architecture document. Update when you change architecture.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Three-role agent model\n     6\t\n     7\tDarkfactory uses three agent roles, each running as a separate AI conversation:\n     8\t\n     9\t**Orchestrator** -- the main Claude Code session. Plans the work, dispatches one worker at a time, runs validators, manages fix loops and circuit breakers. Uses plan mode to keep instructions alive across long conversations. Defined by `skills/mission/SKILL.md` (10 phases, 0-9).\n    10\t\n    11\t**Workers** -- each builds a single task from a brief. Default engine is Codex (via `darkfactory-dispatch`); Claude is an alternative. A worker receives only its brief, never the contract. Makes changes in one commit and writes a handoff report.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Validators** -- check each worker's output. Two run back-to-back per task: scrutiny (build, test, lint, code review, contract check, execution check, test health) and user-test (tests the feature as a user would). Validators see the contract and the code, never the proposal or design.\n    14\t\n    15\t## Information walls\n    16\t\n    17\tWorkers never see the contract. Validators never see the design.\n    18\t\n    19\tThis forces validation to test what the code *does*, not what someone *intended*. Fix workers receive symptom descriptions, never the contract, so they solve real problems instead of gaming a checklist.\n    20\t\n    21\t## File-backed state\n    22\t\n    23\tAll state lives on disk, not in memory. Sessions can crash and resume. Progress is visible from a second terminal.\n    24\t\n    25\t```\n    26\topenspec/changes/<change-name>/\n    27\t  proposal.md              # What and why\n    28\t  specs/<cap>/spec.md      # WHEN/THEN requirements\n    29\t  design.md                # Architecture decisions, module inventory, type catalog\n    30\t  tasks.md                 # Ordered checklist with topology metadata\n    31\t  coverage-audit.md        # PRD-to-task traceability\n    32\t  contract.md              # Observable behavior checks, traced to specs\n    33\t  mission/\n    34\t    config.yaml            # Engine per role (codex or claude)\n    35\t    progress.md            # Running log\n    36\t    briefs/task-N.md       # What each worker receives\n    37\t    handoffs/task-N.md     # What each worker reports\n    38\t    validations/           # Validator reports\n    39\t    issues/                # Failure details per attempt\n    40\t    logs/                  # Streaming output (tail -f)\n    41\t```\n    42\t\n    43\t## Skill structure\n    44\t\n    45\tSkills live in `skills/` at the repo root. `install.sh` symlinks each into `~/.claude/skills/` for Claude Code discovery.\n    46\t\n    47\tEach skill is a self-contained module. Public interface: frontmatter (name, description). Implementation: SKILL.md body. No skill imports from another at the filesystem level.\n    48\t\n    49\tThe mission skill is intentionally monolithic (~900 lines). Companion files are read on demand:\n    50\t- `templates/` -- artifact formats (plan, contract, worker brief, design, etc.)\n    51\t- `prompts/` -- instructions handed to validator and reviewer subagents\n    52\t\n    53\t12 skills total: mission, mission-resume, mission-status, mission-init, grill, language, prd, tdd, test-quality, security, adversarial, dispatch.\n    54\t\n    55\t## Build loop\n    56\t\n    57\tFor each task: dispatch worker, run scrutiny validator, run user-test validator. On failure: diagnostic triage, then fix worker with symptoms only (information wall maintained). Circuit breaker after 3 attempts (1 build + 2 fixes) -- pauses for human decision.\n    58\t\n    59\tAfter task 1 passes, a foundation audit checks module structure and type names against the design.\n    60\t\n    61\t## Planning constraints\n    62\t\n    63\tDesign phase scores 2-3 architecture candidates on a four-dimension rubric (separation of concerns, testability, task independence, module depth). A mandatory gate verifies the design includes an architecture decision, module inventory, type catalog, error strategy, integration points, and test strategy.\n    64\t\n    65\tBefore task generation, the orchestrator presents a decomposition strategy (layered, vertical, or hybrid) for human approval. Each task carries topology metadata: depends-on, consumes, produces, layers.\n    66\t\n    67\t## Configuration\n    68\t\n    69\tGlobal defaults in `~/.darkfactory/config.yaml`. Per-mission engine assignments in `mission/config.yaml` (worker, scrutiny_validator, usertest_validator, adversarial reviewers).\n    70\t\n    71\t## External dependencies\n    72\t\n    73\t- Claude Code (orchestrator, Claude workers, validators)\n    74\t- Codex CLI (Codex workers, adversarial reviewer)\n    75\t- darkfactory-dispatch (Rust supervisor: process isolation, timeout, heartbeat, structured results)\n    76\t- OpenSpec CLI (planning artifact generation)\n    77\t- GitHub CLI (PRD issues, draft PRs)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQE4EJ2EGRYEET558ZVNHM9F","ts":"2026-04-30T02:46:52Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md | sed -n '1,170p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Ubiquitous Language\n     2\t\n     3\t## Mission system\n     4\t\n     5\t| Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n     6\t| --- | --- | --- |\n     7\t| **Mission** | An autonomous run of the full plan-build-validate cycle for a single OpenSpec change | Job, run, session, task |\n     8\t| **Orchestrator** | The main Claude Code session that plans work, dispatches workers, reads results, and decides what to do next | Coordinator, manager, controller |\n     9\t| **Worker** | A subagent (default: Codex) that implements a single task and commits the result | Builder, implementer, coder |\n    10\t| **Validator** | A Claude subagent that checks a worker's output against the contract without having seen the code before | Reviewer, checker, tester |\n    11\t\n    12\t## Planning and contracts\n    13\t\n    14\t| Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n    15\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    16\t| **Contract** | A set of assertions written during planning that define \"done\" independently of implementation, consumed only by validators | Test plan, acceptance criteria, spec |\n    17\t| **Assertion** | A single verifiable statement in a contract that a validator can check from outside the code | Test case, requirement, check |\n    18\t| **Brief** | A self-contained markdown file the orchestrator writes for a worker, containing the task description, project context, and procedures | Prompt, instructions, spec |\n    19\t| **Handoff** | A structured report a worker writes to disk after completing a task, including the commit SHA, approach taken, and any concerns | Report, summary, output |\n    20\t| **Vertical slice** | The default task quality standard. Each task must produce a concrete, testable output. When the approved strategy is vertical, tasks cross all layers. When layered or hybrid, tasks may be scoped to a single layer with a clear done state and test surface. | Horizontal slice, layer |\n    21\t| **Deviation classification** | Categorization of spec deviations during the coverage audit as Interpretation (ambiguous spec, reasonable reading), Substitution (replacing specified approach with a different one), or Removal (not implementing a specified feature). Substitutions and removals require explicit human approval. | Deviation type, deviation level |\n    22\t| **Spec delta check** | A post-build verification step (Phase 9) where a Claude subagent checks that every item marked COVERED in the coverage audit is actually present in the built codebase. APPROVED-DEVIATION items are listed but not re-litigated. | Final spec check, compliance check, coverage verification |\n    23\t\n    24\t## Validation\n    25\t\n    26\t| Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n    27\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    28\t| **Scrutiny validator** | The first validator type that runs build, test, lint, code review, code simplify, and test health checks against the contract | Code reviewer, linter, static validator |\n    29\t| **User-test validator** | The second validator type that tests behavior by hitting the API (HTTP tests) or driving a browser (agent-browser) | QA, E2E tester, integration tester |\n    30\t| **Behavioral conformance** | A scrutiny validator check (check 11) that verifies observable behavior by writing and executing a test script rather than reading the code. Falls back to \"verified by inspection\" if execution is inconclusive. | Behavioral test, execution test, runtime check |\n    31\t| **Final gates** | Whole-repo build, test, lint, and format checks run after the last task completes but before PR creation. Catches regressions introduced by later tasks (e.g., documentation or CI tasks) after the cleanup task ran. | Final checks, post-build gates, repo gates |\n    32\t| **Module shape re-check** | A mandatory closing task that compares design.md module descriptions and Type Catalog names to what the code actually exposes, catching cumulative drift across tasks that per-task scrutiny cannot see | Shape check, surface audit, drift check |\n    33\t| **Demo video** | A recording of frontend functionality captured after both validators pass, created for human review | Screencast, screen recording |\n    34\t| **Fix loop** | The cycle where a validation failure spawns a fix worker, then both validators re-run, up to 3 attempts before asking the human | Retry, repair cycle |\n    35\t| **Circuit breaker** | The rule that stops the fix loop after 3 failed attempts on the same task and asks the human for guidance | Retry limit, fail-safe |\n    36\t\n    37\t## Test health\n    38\t\n    39\t| Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n    40\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    41\t| **Silent skip** | A test that conditionally returns early without testing anything but reports PASS to the test runner, hiding missing coverage | Conditional skip, soft skip, hidden skip |\n    42\t| **Diff coverage** | Line coverage measured only on added lines in a task's git diff, not the entire codebase | New code coverage, incremental coverage |\n    43\t| **Error handling smell** | A language-specific anti-pattern in error handling code (e.g., `.unwrap()` in Rust, `catch {}` in TypeScript, `except: pass` in Python) counted per KLOC of changed code | Code smell, anti-pattern, bad practice |\n    44\t| **File size pre-pass** | A deterministic line count on touched files injected into the module depth review, ensuring the LLM reviewer cannot overlook large files | Line count check, size gate |\n    45\t| **Test quality review** | An LLM-based semantic review (owned skill: `/test-quality`) that evaluates whether tests verify meaningful behavior, checking for tautological assertions, implementation coupling, untested branches, and missing edge cases | Test review, test audit, test assessment |\n    46\t| **Tautological assertion** | A test assertion that passes regardless of whether the code works correctly (e.g., `assert!(true)`, `expect(x).toBeDefined()` without checking the value) | Empty assertion, no-op test, fake test |\n    47\t| **Self-gating check** | A scrutiny check that naturally skips itself when the diff contains no relevant files, requiring no explicit closing-task exemption | Auto-skip, conditional check |\n    48\t\n    49\t## Execution model\n    50\t\n    51\t| Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n    52\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    53\t| **Serial execution** | The rule that only one worker runs at a time to prevent conflicts | Sequential, single-threaded |\n    54\t| **Engine** | The configurable AI model/tool used for a given role (e.g. Codex for workers, Claude for validators) | Model, provider, backend |\n    55\t| **Plan mode** | The Claude Code plan that serves as the orchestrator's durable operating manual, guaranteed to survive context compaction | System prompt, instructions |\n    56\t\n    57\t## File artifacts\n    58\t\n    59\t| Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n    60\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    61\t| **Mission directory** | The `mission/` folder nested inside an OpenSpec change directory that holds all runtime state for a mission | State folder, workspace |\n    62\t| **Progress log** | The `progress.md` file where the orchestrator records task status, decisions, and issues as the mission runs | Status file, mission log |\n    63\t| **Mission config** | The `config.yaml` file specifying which engine to use for each role in the mission | Settings, preferences |\n    64\t\n    65\t## OpenSpec integration\n    66\t\n    67\t| Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n    68\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    69\t| **Change** | An OpenSpec unit of work with its own proposal, design, tasks, and now contract and mission directory | Feature, epic, project |\n    70\t| **Proposal** | The OpenSpec artifact describing what is being built and why | PRD, requirements doc |\n    71\t| **Design** | The OpenSpec artifact describing how it will be built (architecture decisions) | Architecture doc, technical spec |\n    72\t| **Tasks** | The OpenSpec artifact listing tasks as checkboxes, each representing one worker's worth of work (should follow the approved task quality standard) | Checklist, todo list, backlog |\n    73\t\n    74\t## Dispatch\n    75\t\n    76\t| Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n    77\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    78\t| **Dispatcher** | The `darkfactory-dispatch` Rust binary that sits between the orchestrator and a Codex worker as a process supervisor | Wrapper, harness, runner, proxy |\n    79\t| **Dispatch** | The act of spawning a supervised Codex subprocess through the dispatcher, including process group setup, timeout management, and structured result reporting | Launch, invoke, run |\n    80\t| **Heartbeat** | A periodic NDJSON event emitted by the dispatcher to stderr, carrying elapsed time and last activity, used to distinguish \"working\" from \"dead\" | Ping, keepalive, pulse |\n    81\t| **Two-phase timeout** | The dispatcher's timeout strategy: a soft timeout sends SIGTERM and arms a grace period (default: half the timeout, minimum 1 second), then a hard timeout sends SIGKILL | Timeout, deadline, kill timer |\n    82\t| **Process group** | An OS-level grouping (via `setpgid`) that ensures killing the Codex process also kills any grandchild processes it spawned | Process tree, child processes |\n    83\t| **Dispatch store** | A minimal filesystem directory at `~/.darkfactory/dispatches/<ulid>/` holding `status.json`, `result.json`, `host.pid`, and `events.jsonl` for async dispatch lifecycle management | State directory, result store |\n    84\t| **Dispatch result** | A structured JSON object emitted on stdout when a dispatch completes, containing status, exit code, signal, duration, output path, token usage, and activity | Result, outcome, response |\n    85\t\n    86\t## Planning determinism\n    87\t\n    88\t| Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n    89\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    90\t| **Deterministic Anchors** | One-liner pointers in the plan to the six highest-leverage design decisions, positioned to survive context compaction | Anchors, plan summary, design cache |\n    91\t| **Architecture Candidate Scoring** | Evaluating 2-3 architecture options against a four-dimension rubric (separation of concerns, testability, task independence, module depth) to select an architecture deterministically | Architecture review, arch comparison |\n    92\t| **Task Independence** | A scoring dimension measuring how well an architecture allows tasks to be completed without cross-task coordination on conventions | Worker task-ability, decomposability |\n    93\t| **Task Topology** | Per-task metadata declaring which prior tasks it depends on, which surfaces it consumes, which surfaces it produces, and which architectural layers it touches | Task graph, dependency map |\n    94\t| **Foundation Audit** | A one-time check after task 1 passes validation, verifying that committed code matches the design's module inventory and type catalog for task 1's declared produces | Post-task-1 check, foundation check |\n    95\t| **Type Catalog** | A design.md section listing domain types with name, purpose, and owning module -- no field-level detail. Type names are drawn from this glossary when a matching term exists; types not in the glossary are named by the worker | Type registry, type list, types.md |\n    96\t| **Domain type** | A type that models the problem space. Excludes technical helpers (CLI parsers, test fixtures, generic containers) unless they are public compatibility surfaces | Helper type, utility type, internal type |\n    97\t| **Module Inventory** | A design.md section listing modules with interface width, what they hide, and why the boundary exists | Module table, module list |\n    98\t| **Design Template** | A reference document defining mandatory design.md sections and scoring rubrics, used by the orchestrator alongside openspec config rules | Design spec, design schema |\n    99\t| **Spec Reference** | A capability/scenario identifier (e.g., add-todo/1) used to trace contract assertions back to formal spec requirements | REQ-ID, requirement ID, spec ID |\n   100\t\n   101\t## Relationships\n   102\t\n   103\t- A **Mission** belongs to exactly one **Change**\n   104\t- A **Change** contains one **Proposal**, one **Design**, one **Tasks** list, one **Contract**, and one **Mission directory**\n   105\t- Each **Task** maps to one **Worker** invocation, one git commit, and one validation cycle\n   106\t- A **Contract** contains **Assertions** grouped by **Task**\n   107\t- The **Orchestrator** writes **Briefs** and reads **Handoffs**\n   108\t- The **Orchestrator** spawns Codex **Workers** through the **Dispatcher**, and Claude **Workers** and **Validators** through the Agent tool\n   109\t- The **Dispatcher** supervises the Codex process: it manages the **Process group**, emits **Heartbeats**, enforces the **Two-phase timeout**, and returns a **Dispatch result**\n   110\t- **Workers** read **Briefs** and write **Handoffs** -- they never see the **Contract**\n   111\t- **Validators** read the **Contract** and the commit diff -- they never see the **Proposal** or **Design**\n   112\t- A **Fix loop** produces versioned **Handoffs** (task-N-fix-v2.md) and re-runs both **Validators**\n   113\t- The **Circuit breaker** halts the **Fix loop** and returns control to the human\n   114\t- The **Dispatch store** is independent of the **Mission directory** -- it holds dispatcher metadata, not mission state\n   115\t- The **Scrutiny validator** runs deterministic checks (build, lint, **Silent skip** detection, **Diff coverage**, **Error handling smell** density, **File size pre-pass**) and LLM checks (code review, module depth, **Test quality review**)\n   116\t- **Diff coverage** measures added lines only -- a worker is responsible for covering the code they wrote, not backfilling pre-existing debt\n   117\t- The **Test quality review** (`/test-quality`) is an owned skill invoked by the **Scrutiny validator** as a subagent, following the same pattern as `/review` and `/simplify`\n   118\t- A **Tautological assertion** is worse than no test because it gives false confidence -- the **Scrutiny validator** treats it as a hard FAIL\n   119\t- **Deterministic Anchors** are populated from design.md during Phase 4.3 and stored in the **Plan** -- they reference the **Type Catalog**, **Module Inventory**, and **Architecture Candidate Scoring** results\n   120\t- The **Type Catalog** draws **Domain type** names from this glossary. **Worker briefs** list relevant **Type Catalog** entries as exact names to use in code. The **Foundation Audit** checks task 1 names against the **Type Catalog**. The **Module shape re-check** verifies all **Type Catalog** names at the end.\n   121\t- The **Foundation Audit** runs once after task 1, comparing committed code against the **Module Inventory** and **Type Catalog** from design.md\n   122\t- **Spec References** in the **Contract** trace back to specific **Spec** capability/scenario pairs (e.g., add-todo/1), replacing arbitrary row numbers\n   123\t- **Task Topology** metadata is written per task in tasks.md and reviewed during Phase 4.4 -- tasks touching more than 2 architectural layers are flagged for human review\n   124\t- The **Design Template** is a reference document in `templates/` -- the actual design.md generation is driven by openspec config rules that require the same sections\n   125\t\n   126\t## Example dialogue\n   127\t\n   128\t> **Dev:** \"I want to add real-time notifications to Toyo. Should I start a **Mission**?\"\n   129\t>\n   130\t> **Orchestrator:** \"That depends on the scope. How many **Tasks** do you think it involves?\"\n   131\t>\n   132\t> **Dev:** \"At least three -- WebSocket setup, notification delivery, and the UI. Each is a **Vertical slice** cutting through the API and frontend.\"\n   133\t>\n   134\t> **Orchestrator:** \"That's a good fit. Let me **/grill** you on the requirements first, then I'll create the **Change** via **/opsx:propose** and write the **Contract** before any **Workers** start.\"\n   135\t>\n   136\t> **Dev:** \"What if the Codex **Worker** hangs during the WebSocket task?\"\n   137\t>\n   138\t> **Orchestrator:** \"The **Dispatcher** handles that. It emits **Heartbeats** every 15 seconds so I can tell if the worker is still active. If no progress happens, the **Two-phase timeout** fires -- first SIGTERM, then after the grace period SIGKILL to the entire **Process group**. I get a **Dispatch result** with status `timed_out` and fall back to a Claude **Worker**.\"\n   139\t>\n   140\t> **Dev:** \"And I can check on progress while it runs?\"\n   141\t>\n   142\t> **Orchestrator:** \"Yes -- tail the **Dispatcher's** log for **Heartbeat** events, read the **Progress log** directly, or run **/mission-status** in another session for a formatted summary.\"\n   143\t\n   144\t## Example dialogue (planning determinism)\n   145\t\n   146\t> **Dev:** \"Two missions against the same PRD produced completely different architectures. How do we prevent that?\"\n   147\t>\n   148\t> **Orchestrator:** \"The **Design Template** now requires **Architecture Candidate Scoring**. I evaluate 2-3 options on four dimensions -- separation of concerns, testability, **Task Independence**, and module depth -- each scored 0-3. No candidate can score 0 on any dimension. If two tie, you pick.\"\n   149\t>\n   150\t> **Dev:** \"What stops the design from using different type names each run?\"\n   151\t>\n   152\t> **Orchestrator:** \"The **Type Catalog** in design.md names every **Domain type** using terms from the ubiquitous language. The **Worker brief** lists the relevant **Type Catalog** entries so the worker uses exact names. The **Foundation Audit** checks after task 1 that the committed code matches. At the end, the module shape re-check verifies all **Type Catalog** names again.\"\n   153\t>\n   154\t> **Dev:** \"And if context compacts mid-mission and you lose the design details?\"\n   155\t>\n   156\t> **Orchestrator:** \"The **Deterministic Anchors** in the plan survive compaction. They're one-liner pointers to the six key decisions -- workspace layout, task strategy, domain types, error handling, test strategy, and chosen architecture. I can re-read design.md if I need the full detail, but the anchors tell me what to look for.\"\n   157\t\n   158\t## Example dialogue (test health)\n   159\t\n   160\t> **Dev:** \"The benchmark shows irontodo-v2 has 97 tests but only 49 actually run. What happened?\"\n   161\t>\n   162\t> **Orchestrator:** \"Two tests are **Silent skips** -- they check for a database URL and `return` early if it's missing, reporting PASS while testing nothing. The other 46 panic on missing PostgreSQL. The **Scrutiny validator** would now catch the **Silent skips** with a deterministic grep and flag them as FAIL.\"\n   163\t>\n   164\t> **Dev:** \"What about coverage? The tests that do run might not even cover the new code.\"\n   165\t>\n   166\t> **Orchestrator:** \"That's what **Diff coverage** checks. It runs the language-appropriate coverage tool and measures only the added lines in the task's diff. Below 70% is a FAIL. Below 80% is a WARNING.\"\n   167\t>\n   168\t> **Dev:** \"And what if the tests do cover the code but the assertions are meaningless?\"\n   169\t>\n   170\t> **Orchestrator:** \"The **Test quality review** catches that. It's an LLM subagent that reads the test diff alongside the production diff and flags **Tautological assertions** -- tests that pass regardless of correctness. A P0 finding like `assert!(true)` is a hard FAIL.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
