`)\nREADME.md:13:Inspired by [Factory's Missions architecture](https://www.factory.ai/) and [Matt Pocock's AI development skills](https://github.com/mattpocock/skills) [... omitted end of long line]\nREADME.md:21:- [Codex CLI](https://github.com/openai/codex) installed (`npm install -g @openai/codex`) and authenticated (`codex login`)\nREADME.md:29:Clone Darkfactory and symlink its skills into your global Claude Code skills directory:\nREADME.md:43:This compiles a Rust binary and copies it to `~/.local/bin/darkfactory-dispatch`. It supervises Codex subprocess dispatches with process group isolati [... omitted end of long line]\nREADME.md:45:Symlinks mean the skills always point to your local clone, so updating is just a `git pull` (see below).\nREADME.md:53:This checks your entire setup and tells you exactly what's missing. It verifies all 12 skills are symlinked, CLI tools (codex, gh, openspec, darkfacto [... omitted end of long line]\nREADME.md:57:Since skills are symlinked to your clone, pulling updates everything:\nREADME.md:90:For each task, the orchestrator sends a worker (Codex or Claude, your choice) a brief describing what to build. The worker writes the code, commits it [... omitted end of long line]\nREADME.md:119:3. **Independent code review** -- two separate reviewers (Codex and Claude, running at the same time) each review all the changes against the full con [... omitted end of long line]\nREADME.md:133:**Workers** -- each worker builds a single task. By default, workers are powered by Codex (OpenAI's coding agent), but you can use Claude instead. Cod [... omitted end of long line]\nREADME.md:173:## Skills\nREADME.md:175:Darkfactory is controlled through skills -- reusable prompt templates you invoke with a slash command in Claude Code.\nREADME.md:189:| `/adversarial` | Dual-agent code review: dispatches a Codex reviewer and a Claude reviewer with identical input, then surfaces disagreements. |\nREADME.md:202:### Other skills used by `/mission`\nREADM